          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old presents with fever, pharyngitis, and generalised lymphadenopathy.</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old presents with fever, pharyngitis, and generalised lymphadenopathy. Which clinical finding would most strongly favour acute retroviral syndrome over infectious mononucleosis caused by Epstein-Barr virus?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-grade fever lasting more than one week</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Prolonged high fever occurs in both acute HIV and EBV mononucleosis and does not reliably distinguish between them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maculopapular rash on the palms with oral ulcers</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Palmar rash and oral ulcers are characteristic of acute retroviral syndrome. EBV mononucleosis does not typically produce either, making this combination a strong clinical distinguisher.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Generalised lymphadenopathy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lymphadenopathy is common to both conditions — it is a shared feature of acute HIV and EBV mononucleosis and cannot distinguish them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Negative monospot test</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A negative monospot excludes EBV but does not confirm HIV. Many infections produce a negative monospot, so a positive distinguishing feature is more clinically useful.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the features: acute retroviral syndrome characteristically produces a maculopapular rash involving the palms and oral ulcers — neither is typical of EBV mononucleosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. The combination of palmar rash and oral ulcers is a textbook clue to acute retroviral syndrome — glandular fever from EBV typically produces neither."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
