          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An infant born to an HIV-positive mother needs to be tested for HIV at 6 weeks o</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An infant born to an HIV-positive mother needs to be tested for HIV at 6 weeks of age. Which test should be used to confirm infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fourth-generation combination assay</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The fourth-generation assay detects antibodies alongside p24 antigen. In infants under 18 months, passively transferred maternal IgG antibodies cause false positives, making this test unreliable for confirming infant infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>NACO Strategy Three rapid antibody tests</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>All three rapid tests in NACO Strategy Three detect antibodies. In infants under 18 months, these will detect maternal antibodies and cannot distinguish them from the infant's own immune response.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>HIV DNA polymerase chain reaction</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Maternal antibodies persist for up to 18 months, invalidating all antibody-based tests. HIV DNA PCR detects integrated proviral DNA in the infant's own cells, providing a definitive diagnosis independent of antibody status.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum p24 antigen alone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While p24 antigen testing avoids the maternal antibody problem, it has lower sensitivity than HIV DNA PCR in infants and is not the recommended confirmatory test in national guidelines.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: maternal IgG antibodies cross the placenta and persist for up to 18 months. Any antibody-based test will be unreliable during this period — HIV DNA PCR is the correct choice."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. HIV DNA PCR bypasses the maternal antibody problem entirely by detecting viral DNA integrated into the infant's own lymphocytes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
